  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "td-03-average-speed-multi-leg-journeys",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Average Speed Across a Journey",
    "description": "Find average speed from total distance and total time, including equal-distance shortcuts, fractional legs, round trips, and stops.",
    "teachingApproach": "Start by contrasting total-distance/total-time with the tempting arithmetic mean. Then separate equal-distance and equal-time cases, extend to three or more legs and fractional journeys, and finish with round trips and elapsed-time averages that include stops. Use convenient assumed distances when fractions make the arithmetic cleaner.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 18, Time and Distance. Approved scope: Teach average speed as total distance divided by total time. Cover the 2xy/(x+y) shortcut for equal distances, arithmetic mean for equal times, multiple equal-distance legs, fractional journey splits, mixed travel modes, round-trip distance recovery, and the effect of stoppage time on whole-journey average speed."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's learn what average speed actually measures.\n\nIt is not usually the average of the printed speeds. It is the whole distance divided by the whole time.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A car travels 60 km at 60 km/hr, then another 60 km at 30 km/hr.\n\nFirst part: 1 hour.\nSecond part: 2 hours.\n\nTotal distance = 120 km\nTotal time = 3 hr.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Average speed = total distance ÷ total time.\n\n120 ÷ 3 = 40 km/hr.\n\nThe simple mean of 60 and 30 is 45, but the car spends more time at the slower speed, so 45 is not the journey average.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
